From 53e51bef58c5b397dcae44baa0bf3f018a33d3ce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Enrico Bartky <enrico.bartky@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 09:58:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] State and enforce the recompile contract, item 4-2a

The tier-2 hook promotes a function by re-parsing its source text as a fresh
top-level script and keeping the delegate that falls out. Section 4 already
called that "a hook, not a tier" because it recompiles the same code the same
way. It does not: a fresh compilation does not reproduce the scope the function
was written in, and two consequences of that were producing wrong answers.

The recompile builds a SECOND function object and installs only its delegate on
the original, so a body that can observe its own function object observes the
copy while the rest of the program still holds the original. Octane's DeltaBlue
is written UnaryConstraint.superConstructor.call(this, strength); after
promotion the name reaches the copy, which has no superConstructor, and the
suite dies with "Cannot get property call of undefined" - 0 of 1 benchmarks
run against 1 of 1 with tiering off. Strictness is inherited rather than
written, so a strict function re-parsed at top level comes back sloppy:
undeclaredGlobal = t threw a ReferenceError before promotion and silently
created a global after it.

Identity is refused, strictness is repaired. TieringRecompileContract declines a
function whose body mentions its own name or `arguments` - the second because
arguments.callee is the function object by a route no name check can see, and
can be reached through an alias. The refusal is asked at the decision point
rather than left to the tiering gate's conjunction, for the reason item 4-3a
records about its own condition 3. The wrapper re-states 'use strict' when the
original was strict, which is the one condition a re-parse can reproduce.

Measured on the seven suites item 4-1 used, with a budget generous enough not to
bound the answer: the branch is reached by real code - 16 to 100 promotions per
suite across five of seven - so the item is well founded, and DeltaBlue now
completes. The refusal costs about 5% of promotions outside DeltaBlue
(195 -> 186); DeltaBlue itself goes 9 -> 44 because it stops dying.

Recursion by name is refused too, and that is the cost of the rule rather than
an oversight: the copy calling the copy computes the same answer, but telling
"invoked" from "observed" needs a use analysis the contract deliberately does
not do. The first draft of the detector inherited AstReduce's treatment of
VariableDeclarator, ObjectProperty and Case as leaves and so admitted a
self-reference hidden in a declarator initializer - the same "did not look reads
as did not find" failure this item is about, one level down. One test per leaf
kind pins it.

Adds RecompileContractTests (19 cases), each running the same source through a
tiered and an untiered context and requiring the two to agree, with a control
that is still promoted so no refusal passes vacuously. Adds --specializing-tier.
Repository suite: 7 807 tests across 13 projects, 0 failures.
